    <section class="msv-section">
      <div class="msv-section-label">FAQs</div>
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Schools in Spain</h2>

      <details class="msv-faq-item">
        <summary class="msv-faq-question">Can my child attend a Spanish state school without speaking Spanish?</summary>
        <div class="msv-faq-answer">Yes. Spanish state schools are required to accept all legally resident children regardless of their language ability. Most schools have structured support programmes for non-Spanish-speaking pupils — called aulas de acogida or apoyo linguistico — which provide intensive Spanish language instruction alongside gradual integration into mainstream classes. Children typically achieve social fluency within 6–12 months of full immersion, though academic Spanish takes longer to develop.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv-faq-item">
        <summary class="msv-faq-question">How do I enrol my child in a Spanish school?</summary>
        <div class="msv-faq-answer">For state and concertado schools, enrolment is managed by the regional education authority (consejeria de educacion), not the individual school. You apply during the annual admissions period (typically February to April for September entry) and list school preferences. Places are allocated based on proximity to home, siblings at the school, and family income. For international schools, you apply directly to the school with rolling intake throughout the year. Your padron certificate (proof of local address registration) is essential for state school applications.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv-faq-item">
        <summary class="msv-faq-question">What documents do I need to enrol my child in a Spanish school?</summary>
        <div class="msv-faq-answer">For state and concertado schools you will typically need: your child's birth certificate (with apostille and sworn Spanish translation if foreign), your child's NIE, a recent padron certificate showing your address, your own NIE or residency documentation, your child's vaccination record (translated if necessary), and previous school reports. International schools may additionally require placement test results and a reference from the previous school. Allow 2–4 weeks to obtain apostille stamps on foreign birth certificates.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv-faq-item">
        <summary class="msv-faq-question">Are Spanish state schools good quality?</summary>
        <div class="msv-faq-answer">Spanish state schools are generally good quality by international standards. Spain performs around or slightly above the OECD average in PISA assessments across reading, maths, and science. Quality varies by region — Madrid and the Basque Country tend to score highest and have the best-developed bilingual programmes. Teaching staff in state schools are permanent civil servants with standardised qualifications. For expat families, the main adjustment is language and cultural style rather than academic quality.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv-faq-item">
        <summary class="msv-faq-question">Is my child entitled to free state school education in Spain?</summary>
        <div class="msv-faq-answer">Yes. All children legally resident in Spain — regardless of nationality — are entitled to free state school education from age 6 (start of primaria) to age 16 (end of compulsory ESO). Pre-school education (educacion infantil, ages 3–6) is also free in state schools and widely available, though places at 3 years of age are not always guaranteed in high-demand areas. Post-compulsory bachillerato (16–18) is also publicly funded and generally free at state schools, with only materials and small activity fees.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv-faq-item">
        <summary class="msv-faq-question">What are the age ranges in the Spanish school system?</summary>
        <div class="msv-faq-answer">The Spanish system has four main stages: Educacion Infantil (voluntary, ages 3–6), Educacion Primaria (compulsory, ages 6–12), ESO or Educacion Secundaria Obligatoria (compulsory, ages 12–16), and Bachillerato (optional, ages 16–18). Children start primary school in September of the year they turn 6. A key difference from the UK system is that there is no separate middle school — children go directly from primary to secondary at age 12.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv-faq-item">
        <summary class="msv-faq-question">What is a concertado school in Spain?</summary>
        <div class="msv-faq-answer">A concertado school is a privately owned school that receives significant government subsidies in exchange for following the state curriculum and capping its fees. The majority are run by Catholic religious orders, though secular concertado schools exist. They typically charge voluntary monthly contributions of around €100–500 plus materials costs. Concertado schools often have smaller class sizes, stronger bilingual programmes, and a more active parent community than some state schools. Admissions are managed through the same regional system as state schools.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv-faq-item">
        <summary class="msv-faq-question">How much do international schools cost in Spain?</summary>
        <div class="msv-faq-answer">International school fees in Spain vary significantly by school and city. British curriculum schools typically cost between 8,000 and 18,000 euros per year in tuition. American curriculum schools range from 10,000 to 22,000 euros. IB schools generally cost between 12,000 and 25,000 euros per year. Most schools also charge a one-off registration fee (€1,000–4,000) and annual materials, activity, and bus fees on top of tuition. Fees are highest in central Madrid and Barcelona; coastal areas and smaller cities tend to be somewhat lower.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv-faq-item">
        <summary class="msv-faq-question">What language support is available for non-Spanish-speaking children in state schools?</summary>
        <div class="msv-faq-answer">Support varies significantly by region and individual school. Madrid provides aulas de acogida (welcome classrooms) where children receive intensive Spanish instruction for several hours daily before gradually integrating into mainstream classes. Andalucia runs ATAL programmes. The Basque Country and Catalonia have their own arrangements. The quality and intensity of support depends on the school's experience with non-Spanish-speaking pupils — ask specifically about their approach during any school visit. In areas with large expat populations (Costa del Sol, Costa Blanca, Madrid), schools tend to have more established routines for this.</div>
      </details>

      <details class="msv-faq-item">
        <summary class="msv-faq-question">What provision is there for children with special educational needs in Spain?</summary>
        <div class="msv-faq-answer">Spain's framework for SEN is built around inclusive education (educacion inclusiva), with specialist support provided within mainstream schools where possible. Children with more significant needs may attend specialist centres (centros de educacion especial). To access state SEN provision, your child needs a formal assessment (dictamen de escolarizacion) by the regional educational psychology service. The accessibility and quality of SEN support varies considerably by region. If SEN provision is a priority, ask the school and the consejeria specifically what is available before committing to an area. International schools vary widely in their SEN capability — ask directly and for a written statement of what they can offer.</div>
      </details>
    </section>

